  "changes": [
    "Add Array1/Array2 emplace_value_with_assignment with FnOnce constructor and mutable-donor assignment callbacks, sharing one temporary-lifetime engine without Clone or Default restrictions.",
    "Validate declared bounds before construction; construct a separate temporary, assign into the existing object, destroy the possibly moved-from temporary, and return the existing mutable cell.",
    "Preserve native constructor failure, early assignment failure, and partial assignment failure traces and target mutations; borrowed external storage and unused buffer tails remain observable.",
    "Array2 validates inherited Array1 storage only after construction, preserving C++17 RHS-before-LHS order when ChangeArray1 has independently shortened the backing storage.",
    "Match native copy-assignment fallback through an explicit copy callback, leaving the temporary donor unchanged before its destruction.",
    "Keep existing emplace_value convenience methods as documented Rust replacement policies; do not silently claim they perform native element assignment."
  ],
  "probe_policy": "Native and Rust payloads record C construction, A move assignment, Q copy assignment, X destruction and R successful return. Rust payloads use injected per-fixture Rc recorders and intentionally do not implement Clone. Native copy fallback has only a const-reference assignment overload. Twenty-two native rows are consumed by thirteen tests; two additional tests check Rust FnOnce ownership and returned mutable-cell behavior.",
  "limitations": [
    "C++ variadic perfect forwarding and automatic overload selection are represented by explicit Rust closures. No automatic selection of arbitrary native move/copy assignment operators is claimed.",
    "Ordinary safe Shared views detach on mutable access. Native writable const-reference aliases, arbitrary overlapping wrapper aliases, callback reentry and retained pointers across exclusive borrows remain outside this interface.",
    "The existing convenience emplace_value methods retain Rust replacement and old-target Drop behavior, which is not the native user-defined-assignment lifetime policy.",
    "Constructor/assignment Rust panics are compared with native exceptions at the defined receiver-state and element-event level; stack internals and constructor subobject cleanup require an appropriate caller callback. Panicking Drop and native throwing-destructor termination remain outside this scope.",
    "Only initialized elements and native-defined bounded arithmetic are claimed. Native uninitialized trivial storage, allocator/type-trait policy, ABI equivalence and lifetime covariance beyond the unchanged representation are not proved.",
    "Original assertions are unchanged. These two partial-domain template bindings do not declare either array class complete; duplicate compiler template children add no credit, and no global inventory or original declaration credit is added."
  ],
